"""More Bible stories for children, in three reading levels. ADD lists (id of the story to follow, story) so each lands in biblical order."""
ADD = []

def s(after, id, title, t, refs, emoji, a, b, c, talk, mem=None, hue=200):
    ADD.append((after, dict(id=id, title=title, testament=t, refs=refs, emoji=emoji, text={'4-6': a, '7-9': b, '10-12': c}, talk=talk, memory=mem, hue=hue)))

s('ruth', 'hannah-samuel', 'Hannah Prays for a Baby', 'OT', ['1 Samuel 1:1-28', '1 Samuel 2:1-10'], '🙏',
  "Hannah wanted a baby more than anything. She went to the house of God and prayed and prayed. She cried, but she trusted God. God heard her. She had a little boy and named him Samuel. Hannah was so thankful. She brought Samuel to serve God, and she sang a song of thanks.",
  "Hannah was sad because she had no children. Each year she went to the tent of worship at Shiloh. One year she prayed so hard, with tears, that Eli the priest thought something was wrong with her. 'I am pouring out my heart to the Lord,' she said. Eli told her to go in peace. God answered her prayer, and she had a son. She called him Samuel, which sounds like 'God has heard'. When Samuel was still small, Hannah kept her promise and took him to serve God at the tent of worship. She sang a song of joy: 'My heart rejoices in the Lord.'",
  "Hannah’s story begins in longing and ends in a song. She does not hide her pain from God; she pours it out. Eli misunderstands her at first, and then blesses her, showing that prayer is sometimes a quiet thing that only God sees. After Samuel is born, Hannah gives him back to God, trusting that the gift is not lost but safe. Her song is a model for Mary’s Magnificat: both praise a God who lifts the lowly and fills the hungry. Catholics see in Hannah a picture of persevering prayer.",
  ['What did Hannah do when she was sad?', 'Why do you think she gave Samuel back to God?', 'What would you tell God about today?'], '1 Samuel 1:27', hue=330)

s('elijah', 'naaman', 'Naaman and the River', 'OT', ['2 Kings 5:1-19'], '🌊',
  "Naaman was a great soldier, but he had a bad skin sickness. A little girl said, 'There is a prophet who can help you.' Naaman went to Elisha. Elisha said, 'Wash in the river Jordan seven times.' Naaman was cross. It seemed too easy! But he did it, and his skin became clean and new. Naaman thanked God.",
  "Naaman was the commander of the army of Aram, and he had a serious skin disease. A young servant girl from Israel told his wife, 'There is a prophet in Samaria who could heal him.' Naaman traveled to Israel with horses, silver and gold. Elisha did not even come out to meet him. He sent a message: 'Go and wash seven times in the Jordan.' Naaman was angry. 'Our rivers are better!' he said. But his servants said, 'If he had asked you to do something hard, you would have done it. Why not try something simple?' Naaman washed, and his skin became like a child’s. He said, 'Now I know there is no God in all the earth except in Israel.'",
  "Naaman is a powerful man, but he needs help, and the one who points the way is a nameless slave girl. He expects a grand cure with a show of power; God gives an ordinary river and a simple command. His pride nearly keeps him from it, until his servants speak up. Jesus later says that, in the days of Elisha, many Israelites had leprosy but only Naaman, a foreigner, was cured (Luke 4:27), showing that God’s healing is for everyone. The Fathers saw the seven washings and the Jordan as a sign of Baptism.",
  ['Who helped Naaman, and why is that surprising?', 'Why did Naaman not want to wash in the Jordan at first?', 'When is it hard to do something simple that God asks?'], '2 Kings 5:15', hue=195)

s('jericho', 'gideon', 'Gideon and the Little Army', 'OT', ['Judges 6:11-16', 'Judges 7:1-23'], '🏺',
  "Gideon was afraid. But an angel said, 'God is with you, brave man.' God said, 'Your army is too big.' So Gideon had only three hundred soldiers. They had trumpets and jars with lights inside. At night they blew the trumpets and broke the jars. The enemy ran away! God showed that he is the one who saves.",
  "Gideon was threshing wheat in secret because his people, the Israelites, were afraid of their enemies. An angel appeared and said, 'The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.' Gideon said, 'But I am the weakest in my family.' God said, 'I will be with you.' Thirty-two thousand men came to fight. God said, 'That is too many, or you will think you won by your own strength.' In the end only three hundred were left. At night they surrounded the enemy camp with trumpets and torches hidden in clay jars. They blew the trumpets and smashed the jars, and the enemy fled in panic.",
  "Gideon starts out hiding and doubting, and God calls him ‘mighty warrior’ before he has done anything. The story turns on a surprising subtraction: the army shrinks from 32,000 to 300 so that no one can claim the victory as their own. The weapons are trumpets, torches and jars, signs of a battle that belongs to the Lord. Paul echoes this when he writes that God’s power is made perfect in weakness (2 Corinthians 12:9). Gideon is still imperfect; he asks for signs more than once. God is patient with him.",
  ['Why did God make Gideon’s army smaller?', 'What makes you feel too small or too weak?', 'What does it mean to trust God’s power instead of our own?'], 'Judges 6:12', hue=40)

s('lost-sheep', 'sower', 'The Sower and the Seeds', 'NT', ['Mark 4:1-20'], '🌱',
  "Jesus told a story about a farmer. He threw seeds on the ground. Some fell on the path, and the birds ate them. Some fell on stones, and they dried up. Some fell in thorns, and they were crowded out. But some fell on good soil, and they grew and grew! Jesus said, 'Listen to God’s word and let it grow in you.'",
  "Jesus told a crowd about a sower who went out to plant seeds. Some seeds fell on the path, and birds came and ate them. Some fell on rocky ground, where the soil was thin, so the plants sprang up quickly but withered in the sun. Some fell among thorns that choked them. But some fell on good soil and produced a big harvest: thirty, sixty or even a hundred times what was planted. Later Jesus explained that the seed is God’s word, and the different soils are the different ways people hear it.",
  "Jesus calls this the key to all his parables (Mark 4:13). The sower is generous, throwing seed everywhere, even where it may not grow, just as God offers his word to everyone. The soils are four ways of listening: hard, shallow, crowded and open. The point is not to judge other people but to look at our own hearts: what keeps the word from taking root in me? The harvest is surprising, far more than any farmer could expect, because the power is in the seed, which is God’s word.",
  ['Which kind of soil do you think you are on most days?', 'What are the “thorns” that crowd out God’s word?', 'How can we make our hearts good soil?'], 'Mark 4:20', hue=120)

s('lost-sheep', 'mustard', 'The Tiny Mustard Seed', 'NT', ['Matthew 13:31-33', 'Mark 4:30-32'], '🌳',
  "Jesus said, 'God’s kingdom is like a tiny mustard seed.' It is one of the smallest seeds. But when it is planted, it grows into a big tree. Birds come and make nests in its branches. Jesus also said it is like a little bit of yeast that makes a whole loaf of bread rise. Small things can grow into big things with God.",
  "Jesus told two short stories about the kingdom of heaven. In the first, a man plants a mustard seed, the smallest of seeds. When it grows, it is bigger than the garden plants, and birds come and make nests in its branches. In the second, a woman mixes a little yeast into a large amount of flour, and in the end the whole lump of dough rises. Jesus was telling his friends that God’s kingdom may start very small, in a few people and ordinary acts of love, but it grows and grows, and no one can stop it.",
  "The disciples were a small group of ordinary people, and the kingdom began with them. Jesus uses images of hidden growth: a seed under the soil, yeast in the dough. The kingdom is not a show of force; it works from within. The mustard tree welcoming birds recalls the prophets’ vision of all nations finding shelter in God’s kingdom (Ezekiel 17:23; Daniel 4:12). Catholics see in this the growth of the Church from a few apostles to every nation. It also invites patience in our own growth.",
  ['What small thing grew into something big in your life?', 'Why does Jesus say the kingdom is like yeast?', 'What small act of love can you do this week?'], 'Matthew 13:31-32', hue=100)

s('zacchaeus', 'bartimaeus', 'Bartimaeus Can See', 'NT', ['Mark 10:46-52'], '👀',
  "Bartimaeus could not see. He sat by the road and asked for help. When he heard that Jesus was coming, he shouted, 'Jesus, have mercy on me!' People told him to be quiet, but he shouted louder. Jesus stopped. 'What do you want me to do?' 'I want to see,' said Bartimaeus. Jesus healed him, and he followed Jesus.",
  "Bartimaeus was a blind man who sat begging by the road outside Jericho. When he heard that Jesus of Nazareth was passing by, he shouted, 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!' Many people told him to be quiet, but he cried out even louder. Jesus stopped and said, 'Call him.' They said, 'Cheer up! He is calling you.' Bartimaeus threw off his cloak, jumped up and went to Jesus. Jesus asked, 'What do you want me to do for you?' 'Teacher, let me see again.' Jesus said, 'Go, your faith has healed you.' At once Bartimaeus could see, and he followed Jesus along the road.",
  "Mark places this healing just before Jesus enters Jerusalem for his Passion. Bartimaeus ‘sees’ who Jesus is, calling him Son of David, before he receives his sight. The crowd tries to silence him, but persistence wins. He throws off his cloak, his one possession, and goes to Jesus. After he is healed, he follows Jesus ‘on the way’, which for Mark means the way to the Cross. The Church has used this story to teach about the prayer ‘Lord, have mercy’, which echoes his cry in the Mass.",
  ['Why did Bartimaeus keep shouting?', 'What do you want Jesus to do for you?', 'What did Bartimaeus do after he could see?'], 'Mark 10:51', hue=210)

s('zacchaeus', 'mary-martha', 'Martha and Mary', 'NT', ['Luke 10:38-42'], '🕯️',
  "Jesus visited two sisters, Martha and Mary. Martha was busy cooking and cleaning. Mary sat at Jesus’ feet to listen. Martha said, 'Tell Mary to help me!' Jesus said, 'Martha, you are worried about many things. Mary has chosen what is best.' Jesus wants us to take time to listen to him.",
  "Jesus came to the village where Martha and her sister Mary lived. Martha welcomed him and was busy with all the work of serving. Mary sat at the Lord’s feet and listened to what he said. Martha came to Jesus and said, 'Lord, don’t you care that my sister has left me to do the work alone? Tell her to help me!' Jesus answered gently, 'Martha, Martha, you are worried and upset about many things. Only one thing is needed. Mary has chosen what is better, and it will not be taken away from her.'",
  "This short story is often read as a contrast between action and prayer, but Jesus does not scold Martha for serving. He names her worry. Hospitality is good; it becomes a burden when it crowds out the one thing needed, listening to him. The Church has long seen in the sisters two parts of every Christian life: service and contemplation, which belong together. Jesus says his word and presence come first. We serve better when we have first listened.",
  ['What was Martha worried about?', 'How can you make time to listen to Jesus?', 'Is it better to serve or to pray? Why do we need both?'], 'Luke 10:42', hue=290)

s('zacchaeus', 'ten-lepers', 'The One Who Said Thank You', 'NT', ['Luke 17:11-19'], '💛',
  "Ten men were very sick. They called out, 'Jesus, help us!' Jesus said, 'Go and show yourselves to the priests.' On the way, all ten were healed! But only one came back. He fell at Jesus’ feet and said, 'Thank you!' Jesus said, 'Where are the other nine?' It is good to say thank you to God.",
  "As Jesus traveled to Jerusalem, ten men with a serious skin disease stood at a distance and called out, 'Jesus, Master, have pity on us!' Jesus told them to show themselves to the priests. As they went, they were healed. One of them, a Samaritan, came back praising God in a loud voice. He fell at Jesus’ feet and thanked him. Jesus asked, 'Were not all ten healed? Where are the other nine? Was no one found to return and give praise to God except this foreigner?' Then he said, 'Get up and go. Your faith has made you well.'",
  "All ten were healed as they obeyed Jesus’ word, but only one turned back to give thanks. He is the one who is least expected, a Samaritan. Jesus points out that gratitude is a mark of faith. The Greek word for ‘give thanks’ here is eucharistein, from which we get ‘Eucharist’. The Mass is the Church’s great thanksgiving. The story invites us to notice how many gifts we receive and to turn back to the Giver.",
  ['Why did only one man come back?', 'Who can you thank today, and how?', 'What does the word “Eucharist” mean?'], 'Luke 17:16', hue=50)

s('zacchaeus', 'widows-mite', 'The Widow’s Two Coins', 'NT', ['Mark 12:41-44'], '🪙',
  "Jesus sat in the Temple and watched people give money. Rich people gave a lot. Then a poor woman came. She put in two tiny coins. Jesus said, 'This woman gave more than anyone. She gave everything she had.' God cares about how much love we put into our gifts.",
  "One day in the Temple, Jesus sat down opposite the place where people put their offerings. He watched the crowd putting in money. Many rich people put in large amounts. Then a poor widow came and dropped in two small copper coins, worth only a few cents. Jesus called his disciples over. 'I tell you the truth,' he said, 'this poor widow has put in more than all the others. They gave out of their wealth, but she, out of her poverty, put in everything she had to live on.'",
  "Jesus values gifts not by size but by sacrifice and love. The rich give from their surplus; the widow gives from her need, trusting God to provide. Mark places this scene just before Jesus predicts the destruction of the Temple and speaks of his own gift of himself. The widow’s total self-giving foreshadows what Jesus is about to do on the Cross. The Church has seen in her a model of generosity and of trust in God’s care.",
  ['Why did Jesus say the widow gave the most?', 'What can you give that costs you something?', 'How is the widow like Jesus?'], 'Mark 12:44', hue=45)

s('last-supper', 'talents', 'The Servants and the Gifts', 'NT', ['Matthew 25:14-30'], '💎',
  "A master went on a trip. He gave three servants some money to take care of. Two servants used it well, and it grew. One servant hid his money in the ground. When the master came back, he was happy with the first two. 'Well done!' he said. Jesus tells us to use the gifts God gave us.",
  "Jesus told a story about a man who went away and left his servants in charge of his property. He gave one servant five talents, another two, and another one, each according to ability. The first two put the money to work and doubled it. The third, afraid, dug a hole and hid the money. When the master returned after a long time, he praised the first two: 'Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many.' The third servant said, 'I was afraid.' The master was not pleased that he had not even tried.",
  "A talent in Jesus’ day was a large sum of money. The master’s trust is a picture of what God gives us: time, abilities, faith and love, according to our capacity. The servants who risk are rewarded with joy and more responsibility; the one who hides his talent does so out of fear, and he has a wrong picture of the master. The parable is not about money but about how we answer God’s trust. The Church has used it to teach about the use of our gifts for the good of others and for the Kingdom.",
  ['What gift has God given you?', 'Why was the third servant afraid?', 'How can you use your gifts to help others?'], 'Matthew 25:21', hue=60)

s('paul', 'philip-ethiopian', 'Philip and the Traveler', 'NT', ['Acts 8:26-40'], '📜',
  "An angel told Philip, 'Go to the desert road.' There he saw a man in a chariot, reading from the Bible. 'Do you understand what you are reading?' asked Philip. 'How can I, unless someone explains?' said the man. Philip told him about Jesus. The man asked to be baptized. They went down to some water, and Philip baptized him. The man went home full of joy.",
  "An angel told Philip to go south on the road from Jerusalem to Gaza. There he met an Ethiopian official, the treasurer of the queen, riding in his chariot and reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah. The Spirit told Philip, 'Go and stay near that chariot.' Philip asked, 'Do you understand what you are reading?' The man said, 'How can I, unless someone explains it to me?' He invited Philip to sit with him. Beginning with the passage in Isaiah, Philip told him the good news about Jesus. When they came to some water, the man said, 'Look, here is water. What stops me from being baptized?' Philip baptized him. Then the Spirit took Philip away, and the official went on his way rejoicing.",
  "This is one of the first stories of the Church reaching beyond Israel. The Ethiopian official is an outsider on many counts, but he is searching, reading Isaiah 53 about the suffering servant. Philip’s way of teaching is a model: he listens, asks a question, starts where the person is, and explains Scripture in light of Jesus. The result is Baptism, and joy. The Church has long used this passage to show that Scripture needs a guide, and that the Word leads to the sacraments.",
  ['What was the man reading, and why did he need help?', 'How did Philip start the conversation?', 'Who has helped you understand the Bible or the faith?'], 'Acts 8:35', hue=170)

s('paul', 'paul-silas', 'Singing in Prison', 'NT', ['Acts 16:16-34'], '🎶',
  "Paul and Silas were put in jail for telling people about Jesus. At midnight they were not sad. They prayed and sang songs to God! The other prisoners listened. Suddenly there was an earthquake, and the doors opened! The jailer was afraid, but Paul said, 'We are all here.' The jailer asked, 'How can I be saved?' He and his family became Christians.",
  "In the city of Philippi, Paul and Silas were arrested, beaten and put into the inner cell of the prison with their feet fastened in stocks. About midnight, they were praying and singing hymns to God, and the other prisoners were listening. Suddenly there was a violent earthquake. The prison doors flew open, and everyone’s chains came loose. The jailer woke up and, thinking the prisoners had escaped, was about to kill himself. But Paul shouted, 'Don’t harm yourself! We are all here!' The jailer asked, 'What must I do to be saved?' They said, 'Believe in the Lord Jesus, and you and your household will be saved.' He washed their wounds, and he and his whole family were baptized that night.",
  "Paul and Silas are in pain and in the dark, yet they pray and sing. Their joy is not in their circumstances; it is in God. The earthquake does not make them run; they stay, and that act of care for the jailer opens the door to his faith. The jailer, who had put them in chains, ends up washing their wounds and feeding them, and the whole household is baptized. The story shows how the Gospel spreads through suffering endured with joy, and how conversion bears fruit in mercy.",
  ['Why were Paul and Silas singing in prison?', 'Why did Paul tell the jailer not to hurt himself?', 'What happened to the jailer and his family?'], 'Acts 16:31', hue=250)
